<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Western Regions</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xiyu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Western Regions was the Chinese name, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, for the lands west of the Yumen Pass, chiefly the oasis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> such as Kucha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karashahr">Karasahr</a>, Khotan and Shule. Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> contested them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> from the 2nd century BCE and set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of the Western Regions in 60 BCE; the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a> reconquered them between the 70s and 90s CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/western-regions.jpg" alt="Western Regions" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: トムル · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Regions_1st_century_BC(en).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Western Regions, from the Chinese Xiyu, was a historical name used in ancient Chinese chronicles between the 3rd century BCE and the 8th century CE for the regions west of the Yumen Pass. It meant most often the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in present-day southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altishahr">Altishahr</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, in particular its easternmost part around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferghana-valley">Ferghana Valley</a>. It was sometimes used more widely for other lands west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and Tianzhu, which in the novel Journey to the West means the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. Because the regions lay astride the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>, they have been strategically significant to China since at least the 3rd century BCE.</p><p>Before the Turkic migrations the peoples of the area spoke two main groups of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>. The oasis <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of Hotan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> spoke Saka, an Eastern Iranian language, while Kucha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> and the Loulan Kingdom spoke Tocharian languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 138 BCE Emperor Wu of the Western Han sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-qian">Zhang Qian</a> as an envoy to the Western Regions to seek an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> confederation. Zhang was held by the Xiongnu for ten years and the mission failed, since the Yuezhi no longer wished to return east, but his travels were a precursor of the long history between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The region was the setting of the War of the Heavenly Horses between Han China and the Greco-Bactrian Dayuan, and was heavily contested during the Han-Xiongnu War until 89 CE.</p><p>The earliest solid Chinese political control began in 60 BCE, when Emperor Xuan of the Western Han created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">Protectorate</a> of the Western Regions, a military administrative office for what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Eastern Han</a> later set up another protectorate, the Chief Official of the Western Regions. In the 7th century the Tang campaigned against the region and regained full control under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anxi-protectorate">Protectorate General to Pacify the West</a>. Chinese influence was lost after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-lushan">An Lushan</a> rebellion, when many local rulers resumed control.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In later centuries the region became a cultural conduit between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, the Muslim world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including in the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. Among its most significant exports were Buddhist texts, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> sutras, carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by traders and pilgrim monks. The Tang monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> crossed it on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and on his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> produced the influential Great Tang Records on the Western Regions. Later Chinese influence varied. In 1542 the Ming scholar Ma Li compiled a record of a route from the Jiayu Pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. In the nineteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> became Russian Turkestan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> under the Qing was known as Chinese Turkestan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western Regions” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>To control trade along the Silk Road the Uyghurs allied with the Sogdian merchants who controlled oases of Central Asia; the Sogdians enabled them to trade in the <b>Western Regions</b> and exchange Chinese silk for other goods, and their trading communities in China gained Uyghur protection in return.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uyghur-khaganate">Uyghur Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…search for them also opened China to the West: Zhang Qian's mission, though it failed to secure an alliance, gave the Han court its first detailed knowledge of Fergana, Sogdiana, Bactria, Parthia and India, and encouraged Emperor Wu of Han to push Chinese power into the <b>Western Regions</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Han dynasty, campaigning from 108 BCE, set up the Protectorate of the <b>Western Regions</b> in 60 BCE, using plots against local rulers, attacks such as that on Kucha in 65 BCE, and the massacre of the whole population of Luntai when it resisted.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It enters Chinese records in the 2nd century BCE as one of the city-states of the <b>Western Regions</b>, alternating between Xiongnu and Han control.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karasahr">Kingdom of Karasahr</a></li><li><blockquote>Kucha, Qiuci in Chinese, was an Indo-European-speaking oasis state between the Tianshan Mountains and the Taklamakan desert, recorded from the 2nd century BCE among the kingdoms of the <b>Western Regions</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kucha">Kingdom of Kucha</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 70s CE the Han general Ban Chao used it as his base for reconquering the <b>Western Regions</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kingdom of Shule</a></li></ul></section>
